The request is exactly `{protocol:2, proof:<full original 12-key inspect receipt>, operationId:<canonical nonnil UUID>, expectedPoolVersion:<safe integer 1..MAX>}`. The acknowledgment is exactly `{protocol:2, request:<complete original request>, status:marked|already-marked|denied|quarantined|closed}`. Private `mark_ack` and `mark_denied` helpers only construct this response; the latter reuses the existing exact closed-cleanup observation.

The public service-only marker reuses completed v2 `parse_capacity_proof`, `take_proof`, `current` and narrow `retire_if_fenced`. Exact stored proof consumption retains original verification/deadlines and owner high-water/zero unsafe-initial-clock behavior; semantic denial consumes once, malformed or mismatched input does not adopt a receipt, and unexpected SQL exceptions/transaction rollback do not guarantee a burn. The exact consumed receipt cannot replay.

Owner-first locking, shared claim/attempt/allocation and exact approval quota-policy/route-pool associations precede shared-pool locking. The global operation lock follows the pool and deliberately uses the unchanged legacy `bfa_p5_capacity_operation:` namespace. Final v2 eligibility follows all quota/pool/operation waits. Current approval policy, quota enabled/units and pool enabled/quarantine/version/limits must pass before marker or already-marked observation. A new marker changes only the existing allocation's operation ID; global uniqueness and immutable allocation identity are preserved. A different fresh inspect proof can observe the same operation only, never authorize repeated external creation.

No new schema, reservation, quota debit, cumulative hold reset, refund, finality inference, old source/function/privilege edit, migration, connected SQL, real Auth/model/voice call or runtime route. Original exact close/reconcile remain unchanged. Runtime/orchestration, deployed Auth, native concurrency and quiesced no-fallback cutover remain unfinished.

